<!-- Section 6: Conclusions -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Outlook</h2>
  <p>Synthesis of findings for spectrum allocation, orbital debris policy, and constellation licensing decisions.</p>

  <h3>1. The LEO Regime Is Overwhelmingly Dominated by a Single Operator</h3>
  <p>SpaceX's Starlink accounts for 67.8% of all active satellites and 71.7% of all LEO satellites. This single-operator dominance has no precedent in the history of space activity and fundamentally reshapes the statistical profile of the orbital environment. Any regulatory or policy analysis that treats LEO as a homogeneous regime must account for the fact that the "average" LEO satellite is a Starlink satellite at ~550 km, 53&deg; inclination.</p>

  <h3>2. Launch Activity Has Entered an Exponential Phase</h3>
  <p>The 90.5% of currently active satellites launched since 2020 reflects a step-change in deployment rates. Annual active-satellite additions grew from 151 (2019) to 4,329 (2025) — a 28.7&times; increase in six years. This trajectory is driven by mega-constellation deployment and is expected to continue as Project Kuiper scales, China's Qianfan and Hulianwang constellations expand, and Starlink adds further shells.</p>

  <h3>3. Orbital Congestion Is Concentrated in Narrow Shells</h3>
  <p>The mean-motion distribution shows that the vast majority of satellites occupy a narrow band between 15.0–15.5 rev/day (~540–580 km altitude). This concentration creates intersection risk for any object transiting this shell. The Sun-synchronous band (~97&deg;, 14.0–14.3 rev/day, ~800–900 km) represents a secondary congestion zone with high traffic from EO constellations and weather satellites.</p>

  <h3>4. The Non-Starlink Fleet Is Diverse but Small</h3>
  <p>Excluding Starlink, the remaining 4,792 satellites span all orbital regimes (83.2% LEO, 12.4% GEO, 2.6% MEO, 1.8% HEO). The "Other/Unclassified" category (2,438 satellites) contains a long tail of national missions, commercial comsats, and rideshare payloads. No single non-Starlink operator exceeds 5% of the total fleet.</p>

  <h3>5. Implications for Policy</h3>
  <ul style="margin-left:20px; color:var(--text-secondary); font-size:0.92rem; line-height:1.8;">
    <li><strong>Spectrum allocation:</strong> The clustering of thousands of satellites in adjacent orbital shells raises spectrum interference questions that ITAU coordination processes must address at constellation scale, not individual satellite scale.</li>
    <li><strong>Debris mitigation:</strong> The concentration of 10,000+ satellites in a narrow altitude band means any fragmentation event would have outsized consequences. Post-mission disposal compliance rates are the single most important metric for long-term sustainability.</li>
    <li><strong>Licensing parity:</strong> New entrants (Kuiper, Qianfan) face an environment where the most desirable orbital shells are already heavily populated. Licensing frameworks may need to consider incumbent density when evaluating new constellation applications.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

